i'm joined by richard mcvicker now he  lost his mother father  and his sister to coven 19 in the space  of just a few weeks back in 2020 and  richard now joins us to tell us his  story richard thank you so much for  coming to talk to us  um you emailed us at sky news following  the first report of what's now called  partygate for want of a better phrase  why did you feel you wanted to email us  and tell your story  because there's obviously lots and lots  of people that have lost people but when  you hear reports on the television  of people disobeying the rules and the  regulations that we've all had to stick  to  and when we as a family went through  losing three members within three weeks  not being able to see them at all in the  hospital not being able to have a  funeral and it's just hurt and it's  still getting hurt for now when you're  still here is on the television  i've been speaking to people today who  have said that they're still coming to  terms with what happened to them during  the pandemic it's something they may  never come to terms with and the  revelations of what have been going have  been has allegedly been going on in  downing street during that time  has brought it all back  talk to me about what you were going  through at that time it started off with  one member of your family and then just  things just snowballed things just got  so much worse talk to me about what was  going on  yes it was just prior to the the  official lockdown on the 23rd of march  my father was admitted to hospital on  the saturday i spoke to my mum on the  sunday which was mother's day and she  was distraught she was tired she was  looking after me dad  and she took a turn for the worse on the  monday not knowing that covered or  anything we were just knew that there  was some kind of  virus or something going on we made the  decision not to drive over to the house  and burning on trent to visit my mum  and then on the tuesday my sister moved  in for the night to look after it and  then on the tuesday uh she was taken to  hospital my mum  and by the saturday she died  so we obviously couldn't go to see her  that week  they did manage to push the parents into  the same room so they could be together  and luckily the only remembrance we've  got at that time of them  is a picture that the nurses took of  them both lying side by side in bed  which  has absolutely destroyed my younger  sister  and the following tuesday my dad passed  away  and then a week later my elder sister  was ill  i facetimed her and she couldn't eat she  couldn't smell she couldn't taste she  got massive headaches for days on end  and  i said do you need to get to hospital  and they said no i'm following the  guidelines i'm following the guidelines  and within a week she collapsed and she  was put into a coma on a ventilator and  within five days she passed away as well  so we haven't we didn't have any time at  all at the hospital no visiting at all  richard i i really don't know what to  say in response to that it's just one of  so many stories we've been hearing here  on sky news and  when you hear people say look the prime  minister breaking the rules having a bit  of cake being with some colleagues in  the garden in the grand scheme of things  it really doesn't matter  why for you does it matter  because i and probably the rest of my  family to the left of us  are probably not grieved properly  we decided not to have any official  funerals because there was a rule of six  and there was obviously four of us  children left with our partners so that  meant eight  and we said we couldn't turn up at the  crematorium with just eight of us  standing six foot apart not being able  to hug each other and and give the send  off to our parents so we said we  wouldn't have that we decided on private  cremations for the three of them at the  later date and the first we had any  connection back with them it was about  two months later when we had the ashes  back in the in the tubes  and we had a we had a local service with  the eight of us in branston in august on  their wedding anniversary as a send-off  really and that was the first time we'd  seen each other as a family since before  they went into hospital in march  and i just feel that we haven't grieved  properly we haven't had the official  send-off and every time i see these  reports on television it just brings it  all back to think i'd have loved 10  minutes without any of them just to say  to her  and you know it doesn't matter how long  it is it just breaks my heart to see it  keep going and on and on and all these  stories coming out  richard we're looking at  a prime minister who is the rule maker  and there is a see great investigation  that has been completed we're waiting  the results of that report we're also  waiting for a criminal investigation to  to run its course and for the report to  come from that  do you need the conclusions of that  report to make your judgment on the  prime minister no no i don't i know  what's happened  whether they get the punishment they  deserve because i'm sure there's plenty  of other people but we've got our grief  we've got to live with it and you know  how long it takes us i have no idea it  could take us years to get over that  sudden death but i don't need a report  i just i'm a manager in my job  and i lead by example and i expect my  staff to do what i ask them to do and  that's the same with the leader of the  country  we all supposed to do what he asked us  to do and obviously they chose not to  richard let me just say once again thank  you so much for telling us your story as  you said you haven't griefed properly we  really appreciate you talking to us and  you've done it with such dignity and  composure i'm not sure i'm quite keeping  it together as well as you and so many  people been through so much in this  pandemic and it means so much to so many  people you talking on their behalf thank  you no problem at all thank you very  much
 

